// Steady income -- the owner's goal of a stable colony (2026-10-03: "stable,
// not big"), tested against a fixed food supply rather than an endless heap.
//
// The nest goal's box and colony (no plants, mister off, 52 ants at column
// 256 at frame 6,000), and food arriving at a fixed rate 30 columns east:
// 40 cells of provisions (38,400 J) every 1,000 frames from the landing on.
// An endless heap (the nest goal) cannot show whether births follow food,
// because food never runs short; a finite one (`boom_bust`) always ends the
// colony. A fixed income is the case where a colony that matched its births
// to its food would settle at a size the income carries, and one that does
// not swings between boom and famine. Run it in `deeptrace` with `food=0`
// so the harness's `top_up` does not add a second supply on top.
//
// **40, calibrated 2026-10-08** for a colony of a few hundred (owner's
// choice): at the first guess of 10 the colony held about 60; at 40 the
// owner's playtest switches hold 190-280 by 130k (4 seeds) and main's
// shipped defaults about 300.
(
    name: "steady_income",
    title: "STEADY INCOME",
    question: "Food arrives at a fixed rate: does the colony settle at the size the income carries, or swing between boom and famine?",
    bed: (
        width: 512,
        height: 320,
        soil_depth: 80,
        ground_y: 160,
        compartments: 1,
        founders: 0,
        species: "grass",
        colonies: 0,
        colony_ants: 52,
        colony_species: "ant",
        predators: 0,
        lamp_spacing: 33,
        seed: 1,
        extra_walls: [],
    ),
    settings: [
        (subject: "the bed", field: "rain", value: 0),
    ],
    placements: [],
    timeline: [
        (at: 6000, every: 0, until: 0, what: Colony(species: "ant", x: 256, count: 52)),
        (at: 6000, every: 1000, until: 0, what: Heap(material: "provisions", x: 286, cells: 40)),
    ],
    horizon: 600000,
)
